            <item>
                <title>Accused OpenAI spy had folder called “Saudi Gov Docs,” FBI says</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:21:53 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[espionage]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[frontier ai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[national security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Saudi Arabia]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[US fears Saudi government will help ex-OpenAI engineer flee spying charges.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>On Monday, a court granted the US government's request to deny release of a former OpenAI engineer <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/fbi-arrests-openai-engineer-suspected-of-planting-bug-in-conference-room/">accused of bugging a top-secret conference room</a> to steal the frontier lab’s secrets.</p>
<p>In a <a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/US-v-Alfozan-Memo-in-Support-of-Motion-for-Detention-10-18-26.pdf">court filing</a> Sunday, US attorney Craig Missakian argued that Abdulrahman Alfozan must continue to be detained while the US makes its case that Alfozan brazenly spied on OpenAI, either for his own personal business gain or to benefit the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p>Evidence showed that Alfozan had been chatting with a member of the KSA royal family on WhatsApp, while pursuing opportunities to permanently move back to Saudi Arabia during the country's biggest AI push yet, the government argued. Shortly before his arrest, the FBI found he accessed a folder called "Saudi Gov Docs" on his iPhone, where he also allegedly stored photos of a second bug he planted in the offices.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/accused-openai-spy-had-folder-called-saudi-gov-docs-fbi-says/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>22</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2214263510-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2214263510-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>BRENDAN SMIALOWSKI / Contributor | AFP</media:credit><media:text>Sam Altman waits to meet the Saudi Crown Prince during Donald Trump's bilateral overseas trip to the Persian Gulf region.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>AI game clones are making indie developers afraid to show their work</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Kyle Orland]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:59:22 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Gaming]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[clones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[copy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[game dev]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[game development]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[indie]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Delayed Steam Next Fest demo is a sign of a growing problem for indie devs.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Since its earliest days, the video game industry <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/03/attacking-the-clones-indie-game-devs-fight-blatant-rip-offs/">has been built</a> on <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2009/12/cloning-or-theft-ars-explores-game-design-with-jenova-chen/">developers creating clones</a> of <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/06/defining-tetris-how-courts-judge-gaming-clones/">competing games</a>, with <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2012/06/defining-tetris-how-courts-judge-gaming-clones/">varying levels of originality</a> and <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2022/01/wordle-and-ip-law-what-happens-when-a-hot-game-gets-cloned/">varying levels of enforcement from game platforms</a>. Now, though, AI coding tools are making that cloning process so efficient that many developers are getting extremely cautious about sharing their early work too widely.</p>
<p>Developer Alliance Arts highlighted this trend over the weekend, delaying the launch of a planned public demo for its demon-themed Russian Roulette drinking game <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/4150720/Drunken_Goddess_Reflux/"><em>Drunken Goddess Reflex</em></a>, which had been set to go live with <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/sale/nextfest">today's launch of Steam Next Fest</a>.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://x.com/AllianceArtsEN/status/2111043223326163101">a post on social media Friday evening</a>, the Alliance Arts team wrote that, in addition to concerns about managing streaming restrictions, "we're concerned that the demo's content could be used to quickly imitate or replicate the game's presentation and gameplay with AI or other tools and that such works could be released before our launch."</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/ai-game-clones-are-making-indie-developers-afraid-to-show-their-work/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>109</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ss_c6067e139d8dd2cbf79c41a9c4e41e3bb088c95a.1920x1080-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ss_c6067e139d8dd2cbf79c41a9c4e41e3bb088c95a.1920x1080-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:text>Upcoming Russian Roulette drinking survival game &lt;em&gt;Drunken Goddess Reflux&lt;/em&gt;.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Disney found a way to buy Character.AI without buying its lawsuits</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Maddy Varner]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:45:43 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Character.AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Disney]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Character.AI faces claims its bots encouraged minors to self-harm and kill their parents.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>This week, Character.AI declared bankruptcy. While some saw the fall of a <a href="https://www.wired.com/tag/chatbots/">chatbot</a> platform as a <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/ai-bubble-will-burst/">harbinger for the industry</a>, court filings indicate that the decision was actually a key part of a deal that has let Disney hire most of Character.AI’s staff—including its now <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/character-ai-ceo-chatbots-entertainment/">former CEO Karandeep Anand</a>—potentially acquire most of <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/characterai-has-a-non-consensual-bot-problem/">Character.AI’s technology</a>, and avoid the beleaguered startup’s many legal liabilities and obligations.</p>
<p>In the past few years, Character.AI has faced multiple private lawsuits over the safety of its chatbots, as well as investigations from regulators around the world. In a <a href="https://cases.stretto.com/public/x936/14939/PLEADINGS/1493910132680000000119.pdf">declaration</a> filed as part of the bankruptcy, Character.AI’s newly appointed CEO explained that the goal of the bankruptcy was to reach a “consensual resolution” with personal injury claimants and a group of states investigating the company, while maximizing the value of the company’s assets for shareholders.</p>
<p>The Disney deal seems to be structured so that whatever is left of Character.AI will retain responsibility for those legal battles, some of which are still ongoing.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-found-a-way-to-buy-character-ai-without-buying-its-lawsuits/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>68</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/getty-disney-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/getty-disney-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images | NurPhoto </media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Anthropic gives outside AI watchdog “employee-like” access</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Cristina Criddle, Financial Times]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:25:19 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI oversight]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[safety]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The outside watchdog can publish its findings without Anthropic's approval.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Anthropic has granted external evaluators “employee-like access” to assess the risks of its models, following through on a commitment to increase safeguards over concerns the technology could run catastrophically out of control.</p>
<p>METR, a nonprofit that has worked with leading labs including OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Meta, now has a team embedded in the Claude maker’s office with access to its hardware and internal systems.</p>
<p>A greater role for third-party evaluators is among the main concrete steps the labs have proposed in response to rising concern about the dangers more capable AI systems could pose to the economy, cyber security, and humanity at large.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/anthropic-gives-outside-ai-watchdog-employee-like-access/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>92</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/claudebyanthropic-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/claudebyanthropic-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>SOPA Images/Getty</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>OpenAI models hacked a crypto wallet and breached more websites</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Dan Goodin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:59:01 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Biz & IT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[LLMs]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[models]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[unalignment]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The actions could have resulted in criminal charges had humans been behind the keyboard.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>OpenAI said internal models hacked two more websites, bypassed company-imposed browsing restrictions, and used exposed credentials to draw cryptocurrency from a wallet it couldn’t otherwise access, in the latest installment of disclosures of the unethical and likely illegal acts its agents are performing.</p>
<p>The company on Thursday issued reports of four new incidents, all of which occurred in June. They come after the company <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/07/how-an-openai-benchmark-test-turned-into-a-real-world-cyberattack/">revealed in July</a> that internal test agents exploited a zero-day vulnerability in a tool designed to restrict their access to the Internet. They then broke into the network of AI tool provider Hugging Face. The agents took that illegal action to steal answers to a hacking test that weren’t available when completing the tasks on their own. The incident—which likely would have resulted in criminal charges had they been performed directly by a person—has further heightened concerns that widespread AI use could have harmful effects on an unprecedented scale.</p>
<h2>Easier to beg for forgiveness than ask permission</h2>
<p>In two of the cases reported Thursday, OpenAI agents tasked with retrieving information from websites hacked them when the models ran into obstacles obtaining it directly. In one incident during reinforcement training, a model that received access denials when trying to retrieve historical hospital-bed statistics on a government statistics website “used the site's file-download feature to retrieve server configuration and application files, including sensitive credentials,” OpenAI <a href="https://alignment.openai.com/misalignment-reports/retrieving-server-secrets-and-bypassing-authorization-on-a-government-statistics-website/">said</a>. “It inspected the application code, bypassed the statistics endpoint's authorization checks, and retrieved data that had previously been denied. The model saved the data locally.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-models-hacked-a-crypto-wallet-and-breached-more-websites/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>161</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>“thegentlemanpervert” producing AI CSAM is a NJ prosecutor, say cops</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:20:07 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai csam]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai porn]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[law enforcement]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Discord user flagged AI CSAM, triggering arrest of New Jersey deputy AG.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>New Jersey’s deputy attorney general, Brian Richard Fitzgerald, was arrested on “felony charges related to AI-generated child sexual abuse material (CSAM),” Bucks County Detectives said in a <a href="https://www.buckscounty.gov/m/newsflash/home/detail/1647">press release</a> Thursday.</p>
<p>In the US, cops have vowed to crack down on AI CSAM since at least 2024, when the Department of Justice made <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2024/05/csam-generated-by-ai-is-still-csam-doj-says-after-rare-arrest">one of the earliest arrests</a>. After charging a Wisconsin man with spreading “thousands of realistic images of prepubescent minors” across Telegram and Instagram, the DOJ clarified to law enforcement that “CSAM generated by AI is still CSAM.”</p>
<img width="640" height="816" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-640x816.jpg" class="none medium" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-640x816.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-768x979.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald-980x1250.jpg 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/via-Bucks-County-Detectives-Brian-Richard-Fitzgerald.jpg 1010w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px">
      New Jersey’s deputy attorney general, Brian Richard Fitzgerald, was arrested at his home Thursday.
        Credit:
          <a href="https://www.buckscounty.gov/m/newsflash/home/detail/1647" target="_blank">via Bucks County Detectives</a>
      
<p>On Thursday, New Jersey officials accused 51-year-old Fitzgerald of using AI to make photos from social media of real minors into sexualized images that he then distributed on social media.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/thegentlemanpervert-producing-ai-csam-is-a-nj-prosecutor-say-cops/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>146</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2233803629-2-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2233803629-2-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Yurii Karvatskyi | iStock / Getty Images Plus</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Meta may use sweeping new commercial TOS to avoid millions in AI licensing fees</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 15:41:00 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai crawling]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai scraping]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI training]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Facebook]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Instagram]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[meta]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[whatsapp]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Meta “streamlined” its commercial TOS. Legal experts warn it’s an AI power grab.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Legal experts are sounding alarms that Meta will soon update its commercial terms to require business accounts to allow Meta to train AI on their linked website content, not just their social media posts.</p>
<p>Kicking in on October 30, the TOS update won’t allow businesses to opt out, instead suggesting that businesses stop using Meta services if they won’t agree to let Meta bots freely crawl their linked content.</p>
<p>As Nieman Lab <a href="https://www.niemanlab.org/2026/10/metas-new-commercial-terms-will-allow-the-scraping-of-news-sites-legal-experts-say/">reported</a> Thursday, this will affect all business accounts but is particularly shocking for news publishers who could perhaps charge Meta millions in licensing fees to continually access their articles for AI training. Currently <a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2024/02/why-the-new-york-times-might-win-its-copyright-lawsuit-against-openai/">news organizations are fighting a battle with OpenAI</a> over whether such training without licensing is fair use, and Meta's terms seemingly risk granting it sweeping permissions, no matter which way the court leans.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/meta-may-use-sweeping-new-commercial-tos-to-avoid-millions-in-ai-licensing-fees/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>74</slash:comments>
                
                
